__notes__.note.singularity.setting-paradox-restate:
Hawking's argument: general relativity drives information into the singularity and destroys it. Quantum mechanics: information cannot be destroyed. The chain's finding: both statements are true, at different levels of description, and the contradiction is a category error.

\begin{center}
\emph{Information is lost in $\Cl(1,3)$. Information is preserved in $\Cl(2,4)$. These are the same physics read at two levels, and the discrepancy between the levels is not destruction: it is the fixed projection of Proposition \claimref{singularity.descent-what-it-erases}, extended to the boundary mode space by Definition \claimref{obj.singularity.multi-mode-descent}, and its size is an equation: the relative entropy of Theorem \claimref{singularity.deficit-theorem-cost-descent-exactly}, carried entirely by the correlations of the quanta (Corollary \claimref{singularity.deficit-carried-quanta-correlations}).}
\end{center}

General relativity is right that at the metric level the information is not conserved. Quantum mechanics is right that at the full level it is. The deficit between the two levels is not unaccounted for: traced through Theorem \claimref{singularity.modular-descent}, it is precisely the Hawking flux, thermal at $\TH$, the shadow of the entanglement with the adjoined partner; and its magnitude is not estimated but computed, $\rel{\rho}{\EN\rho} = C(\rho) - C(\EN\rho)$, the correlations the descent cannot carry (Corollary \claimref{singularity.deficit-carried-quanta-correlations}; the section “The descent, applied: the two levels in numbers” evaluates it for the model in exact arithmetic). The paradox does not need resolving; it needs the two levels computed separately. What remains a genuine question is the modern sharp form: whether the exterior radiation is unitary \emph{by itself} and follows the Page curve, i.e.\ whether the projection's deficit is returned to the radiation before the end. That question this section takes as far as the two levels allow.

scrambling.candidate-principles-their-yi.formal:
Let a calibration principle be a rule assigning to the exceptional set $E = \{\tau = 0\} \times \R_t \times S^2$ a count of boundary-mode fermions per modular period. Then:
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(P1)] \emph{one fermion per Planck cell of the slice} returns $N_{\mathrm{epoch}} = 16\pi\,\SBH$, and the coefficient is scheme-bound: the slice count scales as $c_0^{3/2}$, so the proper-time scheme returns exactly $\tfrac32$ times the curvature scheme's count and the Kretschmann scheme $12^{1/4}$ times it, an irrational multiple;
\item[(P3)] \emph{one fermion per Planck cell of the sphere at horizon normalisation, once per modular period} returns $N_{\mathrm{epoch}} = A = 4\,\SBH$, independently of the cutoff scheme, because the horizon-normalised coframe on $E$ is exactly $(dt,\ r_h\,d\theta,\ r_h\sin\theta\,d\varphi)$ (Fact \claimref{scrambling.imported-collars-coframe-exceptional-set-helicity});
\item[(P2)] \emph{fundamentals per sphere aggregated over the epoch} breaks the $M$-scaling (Proposition \claimref{scrambling.per-slice-verification-two-failed-aggregations}).
\end{enumerate}

scrambling.clock-inventory-boundary-mode.formal:
Every clock of the boundary modes runs at the modular rate $\kappa$, with period $\beta = 2\pi/\kappa = 8\pi M$. Precisely:
\begin{enumerate}
\renewcommand{\labelenumi}{(\roman{enumi})}
\item Call a flow \emph{intrinsic} when it is a one-parameter group of inner automorphisms of the boundary-mode algebra, with even generator, preserving the vector grade. The intrinsic flows of a Clifford boundary-mode algebra are rotor (bivector-generated) flows, quadratic in the pair operators.
\item Call an intrinsic flow a \emph{clock} when it is hyperbolic (its adjoint action on the mode operators semisimple with real eigenvalues) with a common rapidity across the modes it moves, the rapidity being the parameter the geometric normalisation converts to Schwarzschild time. The algebra fixes only the orbits, not the speed (Fact \claimref{scrambling.imported-modes-modular-inventory}'s reparametrisation clause), so a clock's parameter is normalised to the rapidity itself, not a multiple of it. For a single completed pair the bivector space is one-dimensional, its flow is the boost, and the conversion runs at the surface gravity $\kappa = 1/4M$.
\item A nonzero bivector generates a clock exactly when its adjoint action on the mode operators is semisimple with real eigenvalues, all nonzero ones of equal magnitude, which the normalisation to rapidity sets to one; if $\operatorname{ad}_X$ is semisimple with spectrum $\{+c, 0, -c\}$ then the $\pm c$ eigenspaces are isotropic and mutually dual for the neutral form of the boundary modes, so by Witt extension $X$ is $O(n,n)$-conjugate to $c$ times a sum of standard atom boosts; the geometric normalisation, being a property of the quadratic space rather than of a choice of modes, is invariant under that conjugation and converts every such flow at the one $\kappa$. Writing $E_{ij} = \psi_i^\dagger\psi_j$ for the gauge-invariant bilinears of the $n$-mode boundary space's mode operators $\psi_i$ (Proposition \claimref{scrambling.boundary-mode-space-matrix}, proved independently of this lemma), the cases below are illustrative rather than a partition. The diagonal $E_{ii}$ are the atoms' boosts (Fact \claimref{scrambling.imported-modes-modular-inventory}) and the equal-coefficient sum $\sum_i E_{ii}$ their simultaneous boost, all with adjoint eigenvalues $\pm 1$; the symmetric combinations $E_{ij} + E_{ji}$ are opposed pairs of boosts of the rotated modes $(\psi_i \pm \psi_j)/\sqrt{2}$, again $\pm 1$. The antisymmetric $E_{ij} - E_{ji}$ are compact and the strictly off-diagonal $E_{ij}$ nilpotent, neither carrying a rapidity; a mixed diagonal $\sum_i c_i E_{ii}$ with unequal $|c_i|$ has no common rapidity. And the pairing sector $c\,\psi_i^\dagger\psi_j^\dagger + d\,\psi_j\psi_i$, outside $\mathfrak{gl}(n)$ and completing the bivector algebra of the completed boundary mode space, of dimension $n(2n-1)$ against $\mathfrak{gl}(n)$'s $n^2$ (the $n$ hyperbolic pairs of Fact \claimref{tower.witt-index-quantum-capacity} make the quadratic space split, so that algebra is $\mathfrak{so}(n,n)$), is classified by the sign of one product, its adjoint eigenvalues being $\pm\sqrt{cd}$: hyperbolic for $cd > 0$ (a clock, at unit rapidity outright when $cd = 1$), compact for $cd < 0$, nilpotent for $cd = 0$.
\end{enumerate}
The boundary mode space therefore has many intrinsic flows and one clock rate: whichever clock is read, single-atom, rotated-pair, common, or paired, its period is $\beta$.

scrambling.exact-factorisation-thinning.formal:
In Planck units, with $r_h = 2M$, $A = 4\pi r_h^2 = 16\pi M^2$, $\beta = 8\pi M$ and $\SBH = A/4$:
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(i)] the slice count per unit Killing time is $8\pi M = 4\pi r_h = A/r_h$, at leading order in $r_c/2M$;
\item[(ii)] the slice count per modular period is $N_{\mathrm{slice}} = 64\pi^2 M^2$ and $N_{\mathrm{slice}}/\SBH = 16\pi$, at the same order;
\item[(iii)] $16\pi = (\beta/r_h) \times (A/\SBH) = 4\pi \times 4$, and the first factor is universal for Schwarzschild, $\beta/r_h = 2\pi/(\kappa r_h) = 4\pi$ with $\kappa = 1/(2r_h)$.
\end{enumerate}

scrambling.integer-holographic-form-iden.formal:
With the epoch fixed at $\beta$:
\begin{enumerate}
\item the counted three-volume of one epoch is $V = (8\pi M)\cdot\beta = 64\pi^2M^2$ in Planck units; restoring the length, $V = \beta^2\lP$: the coincidence with $\beta^2$ is a consequence of the framework's units, one visible power of $\lP$ marking the volume as geometric rather than purely modular;
\item the prediction $\nu = 1/16\pi$ holds if and only if the number of boundary modes registered per modular period is
\[
N_{\mathrm{epoch}} \;=\; \nu\,V \;=\; 4\pi M^2 \;=\; \SBH
\]
exactly as arithmetic: \emph{one boundary-mode fermion per four Planck cells of horizon area, per modular period}. The transcendental density becomes an integer-holographic statement, in the 1-nat convention and inheriting the per-mode $\ln 2$ normalisation of Remark \claimref{note.scrambling.density-factor-stated-full};
\item the naive one-mode-per-volume-cell reading ($\nu = 1$) overshoots it by exactly $16\pi$, which is precisely the horizon-area coefficient $A = 16\pi M^2$: the thinning the weighted completion must produce is the area coefficient itself. (a figure of the original draws the assembled counting geometry.)
\end{enumerate}

scrambling.residue-each-convention.formal:
Write the epoch count as $N_{\mathrm{epoch}} = \kappa_c \times A \times 1$, horizon-normalised sphere cells times one tick per modular period, with $\kappa_c$ the dimensionless \emph{calibration residue}, a symbol distinct from the surface gravity $\kappa$ of the section “Setting and imports”. Then:
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(i)] in the 1-nat convention of this paper, $N_{\mathrm{epoch}} = \SBH$ gives $\kappa_c = \SBH/A = \tfrac14$;
\item[(ii)] in the capacity convention of the singularity paper, $N_{\mathrm{epoch}}\ln 2 = \SBH$ gives $\kappa_c = 1/(4\ln 2)$: one boundary mode per $4\ln 2$ Planck areas of horizon per modular period, which is one mode per Bekenstein-Mukhanov area quantum \cite{BekensteinMukhanov1995};
\item[(iii)] the two differ by exactly $\ln 2$, and only (ii) compares an entropy with an entropy: a fermionic mode carries at most $\ln 2$ nats, so the 1-nat convention credits each mode with more entropy than it can hold, and the exact ``$16\pi$'' of Theorem \claimref{scrambling.epoch-modular-period-two-unknowns-become} is a property of that convention, the physical thinning being $16\pi\ln 2$;
\item[(iv)] one boundary mode registers one helicity: by Fact \claimref{scrambling.imported-collars-coframe-exceptional-set-helicity} the two helicities of a massless species are two channels of the lift, so the capacity per boundary mode is $\ln 2$ and not $2\ln 2$, and $\kappa_c$ is $1/(4\ln 2)$, not $1/(8\ln 2)$.
\end{enumerate}

singularity.eviction-window-thermal-prod.formal:
Under (L), (U), (C), (I) and (L$_E$), the window dynamics diagonal in the joint number basis and the radiation quanta free and never returning, with the no-attic property supplied by Proposition \claimref{singularity.no-attic-derived-model}, write $W(t)$ for the set of pairs whose window half is in the window at stage $t$ and $C(t) = |W(t)|\ln 2$ for the capacity. Then, in the rate model:
\begin{enumerate}
\item (The identity.) At every stage the window state is the product of its members' thermal marginals, $\rho_L = \bigotimes_{k \in W(t)}\operatorname{diag}(c_k^{2}, s_k^{2})$, and consequently
\[
S_R(t) \;=\; S_L(t) \;=\; \sum_{k \in W(t)} h_k \qquad \text{exactly.}
\]
\item (Envelope and endpoint.) $S_R(t) \leq \min(\Sth(t), C(t))$ at every stage, and $S_R = 0$ at complete evaporation.
\item (Rising branch, exact.) While no eviction has occurred, $W(t)$ is the set of all injected pairs and $S_R(t) = \Sth(t)$ exactly.
\item (Falling branch, quantified.) $S_R(t) = C(t) - \sum_{k \in W(t)}(\ln 2 - h_k)$: the radiation entropy lies below the capacity by exactly the sum of the window members' per-quantum shortfalls, with equality $S_R = C$ if and only if every window member is maximally entangled. In the maximally entangled idealisation, every $h_k = \ln 2$, this gives $S_R(t) = \min(\Sth(t), C(t))$ exactly at every stage.
\end{enumerate}

singularity.purity-correlation-transfer-.formal:
In the model with two pairs and one controlled-$Z$ between $L_1$ and $L_2$ (the minimal scrambled instance; the script runs larger ones):
\begin{enumerate}
\item the global state is pure at every stage, and at completion the radiation alone carries it: $S(R_{\mathrm{all}}) = 0$ exactly;
\item the pair coherence is damped by exactly the visibility $\gamma = c_2^2 - s_2^2 = \cos 2\theta_2$: the two-qubit marginal of $(R_1, L_1)$ has coherence $c_1 s_1 \gamma$ in place of $c_1 s_1$, so its R\'enyi-2 purity drops from $1$ to $c_1^4 + s_1^4 + 2 c_1^2 s_1^2 \gamma^2$: two-body correlations alone no longer hold the whole pair;
\item the deficit is not lost but relocated to three-body correlations, and it is recoverable: the correlator $\langle X_{R_1} X_{L_1} \rangle$ equals $2 c_1 s_1 \gamma$ while the dressed correlator $\langle X_{R_1} X_{L_1} Z_{L_2} \rangle$ equals $2 c_1 s_1$ exactly, the visibility $\gamma$ having moved from the undressed correlator to the dressed one, so the pattern of applied gates (the message) is determined by multi-qubit correlators although every single-qubit state, by Lemma \claimref{singularity.parity-protection}, is identically thermal.
\end{enumerate}

synthesis.capacity-per-mode-independent-.formal:
A boundary mode is one ladder pair (Proposition \claimref{scrambling.pair-mode-qubit-one-object}): its state space is two-dimensional and its maximal entropy is $\ln 2$, whatever it registers. Hence:
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(i)] Under either reading of the helicity lift the capacity of one boundary mode is $\ln 2$. In the Fock reading a helicity channel's two states are empty and occupied, so two helicity channels have four joint occupation states, that is two ladder pairs; a capacity $2\ln 2$ is the capacity of such a unit of two ladder pairs, which is two boundary modes in the sense of Proposition \claimref{scrambling.pair-mode-qubit-one-object}, not one.
\item[(ii)] Under the hypothesis of Theorem \claimref{synthesis.residue-from-mode-entropy}, group the counted ladder pairs into units of $h$ pairs ($h = 1$: one helicity per unit; $h = 2$: both helicities of a species per unit). The residue per unit is $\kappa_{c}^{(h)} = 1/(4\langle s\rangle_{h}) = \kappa_{c}^{(1)}/h \ge 1/(4h\ln 2)$, with equality exactly when every ladder pair is maximally mixed. The number of ladder pairs per Planck area per modular period, the equality case, and the registered mode density $\nu$ of Theorem \claimref{scrambling.mode-count} (fermionic modes per Planck cell) are the same for $h = 1$ and $h = 2$.
\item[(iii)] Consequently, in the capacity convention and under the hypothesis of Theorem \claimref{synthesis.residue-from-mode-entropy}, the values $\kappa_{c} = 1/(4\ln 2)$ and $1/(8\ln 2)$ of the capacity convention (Proposition \claimref{scrambling.residue-each-convention}(ii), (iv)) are one count of ladder pairs expressed in two units, not two physical alternatives, and the value per boundary mode, $1/(4\ln 2)$, follows from Proposition \claimref{scrambling.pair-mode-qubit-one-object} without the channel identification J3. What J3 decides is how ladder pairs group into the $(\omega, \ell, m, s)$ channels of a species, and so whether the horizon's entropy is matched by one or two helicity channels per $(\omega, \ell, m)$, a field-theory question this result does not settle.
\end{enumerate}

synthesis.central-label-carries-mass-at-.formal:
Assume (P) and (R) as in Theorem \claimref{synthesis.quarter-from-temperature}, so that a boundary mode of Killing weight $\omega$ is thermal at $T(M) = 1/8\pi M$ with occupancy $n = 1/(e^{x} + 1)$, $x = 2\pi\omega/\kappa$, and entropy $s(x)$ (Theorem \claimref{synthesis.boundary-mode-thermal-entropy}).
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(i)] (the tension does not depend on the ordering) with the normal-ordered generator $\omega N$ a mode carries $\beta E = x\,n < s(x)$; with the symmetric generator $\omega K = \omega(N - \tfrac12)$ of Proposition \claimref{hawking.modular-structure-completed-pair}(4) it carries $\beta E = x(n - \tfrac12) = -\tfrac{x}{2}\tanh\tfrac{x}{2} \le 0 < s(x)$. Under both, clause (v) of Theorem \claimref{synthesis.residue-from-mode-entropy} holds, $E < TS = M/2$, and $E = 0$ exactly at zero weight;
\item[(ii)] (the model) let $\mathcal{H} = \bigoplus_{m \ge 0}\mathcal{H}_{m}$, with $\mathcal{H}_{m}$ the Fock module of $m$ completed pairs (dimension $2^{m}$; Proposition \claimref{scrambling.boundary-mode-space-matrix}), every pair of Killing weight zero, and let the Hamiltonian be the central element $H = \mu(\hat m)$, $\hat m = m$ on $\mathcal{H}_{m}$, with $4\pi\mu(m)^{2} = m\ln 2$. On $\mathcal{H}_{m}$ the counted modes' Killing generator vanishes, so their Killing excitation energy is $0$ under either ordering; (R) at zero weight fixes each marginal at $n = \tfrac12$, and with the joint state $2^{-m}\mathbf{1}$ the counted modes carry $S = m\ln 2$. With $M := \mu(m)$ and $A = 16\pi M^{2}$, $S = A/4$, which is the equality case of Theorem \claimref{synthesis.residue-from-mode-entropy}(ii), and the microcanonical entropy at energy $M$ is $\ln\dim\mathcal{H}_{m} = 4\pi M^{2}$, so along the family $dS/dM = 8\pi M = 1/T(M)$, $S \to 0$ as $M \to 0$, and $M = 2TS$. The hypotheses of Theorem \claimref{synthesis.quarter-from-temperature} (a thermodynamic system of internal energy $M$ at $T(M)$ obeying Clausius) and those of the equality case of Theorem \claimref{synthesis.residue-from-mode-entropy} (counted modes carrying $A/4$, all of weight zero) hold together. The internal energy is the value of the central label, which commutes with every counted mode operator and is invisible to their Killing flow; each added counted mode raises it by $dM = T\ln 2$. At fixed $M$ the sector $\mathcal{H}_{m}$ with the state $2^{-m}\mathbf{1}$ is the zero-weight case of the canonical reading filed alongside (synthesis.hole-mass-is-ground-energy-label), a Hamiltonian equal to $M$ on a space of dimension $2^{|J|}$, so this model realises that reading's c-number label explicitly;
\item[(iii)] (microcanonical only) $H$ has no Gibbs state: $\sum_{m} 2^{m}e^{-\beta\mu(m)}$ diverges for every $\beta > 0$, since $\mu(m) < \sqrt{m}/4$, and $dM/dT = -8\pi M^{2} < 0$; this is the negative heat capacity that the canonical reading meets as the statement that no fixed Hamiltonian is Gibbs along the family;
\item[(iv)] (moved modes do not change the carrier) a counted mode at $x = 2\pi$ carries $\beta E/s(2\pi) = 2\pi n(2\pi)/s(2\pi) \in (0.8625, 0.8627)$ of its entropy share in normal order, so at any mixture of zero-weight modes and modes at $x = 2\pi$ the counted modes carry less than $TS$ and the remainder of $M$ is carried elsewhere.
\end{enumerate}
So the carrier of $M$ can be a central label, a function of the number of counted modes, with the counted modes at zero weight; what follows from this for the registered residue is drawn in the canonical reading filed alongside. The function $\mu$ is fixed by the area law, so the model is a consistency statement about the area law, not a derivation of it, and it does not show that the series' boundary modes realise it.

synthesis.counted-unit-energy-at-half-of.formal:
Assume (Q) version 3 (Premise \claimref{singularity.q-joint-quasi-freeness-s-wave}): one s-wave array $\{\psi_{k}\}_{k \in \Z}$ has weights $\omega_{k} = \kappa(k + \tfrac12)$ and is in the quasi-free state at $\beta = 2\pi/\kappa$, so that mode $k$ has $x_{k} = 2\pi(k + \tfrac12)$ and occupation $n(x) = 1/(e^{x} + 1)$ above the sea (holes below it); a counted unit, the bookkeeping unit of the total horizon count per light ray, is two arrays, carrying $\ln 2$ (Theorem \claimref{synthesis.half-integer-weight-array-carries-half-ln2}(i), (iii)). Measure the Killing energy $E$ from the filled sea (the normal-ordered generator, ground energy $0$). Then:
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(i)] (one array) $\beta E = 2\sum_{k \geq 0}x_{k}n(x_{k}) = \pi/12$ exactly, that is $E = \kappa/24$, by the imported theta-function identities (Fact \claimref{synthesis.imported-theta-function-identities}); without them $|\beta E - \pi/12| < 10^{-55}$ by rigorous rational bounds. For the zero offset $\omega_{k} = \kappa k$ the same sum is $\beta E \in (0.02351, 0.02352)$: the value is specific to the half offset;
\item[(ii)] (the sea) the zeta-regularised energy of the filled sea is $-\kappa\,\zeta(-1, \tfrac12) = -\kappa/24$ per array, the finite part of $-\kappa\sum_{n \geq 0}(n + \tfrac12)e^{-\epsilon(n + 1/2)}$ as $\epsilon \to 0$. Hence with the symmetric generator $\sum_{k}\omega_{k}(\psi_{k}^{\dagger}\psi_{k} - \tfrac12)$ of Theorem \claimref{synthesis.hole-mass-is-ground-energy-label}(ii), summed over the whole array and zeta-regularised, one array has $\beta E = 0$ and $\ln Z = \tfrac12\ln 2$, and one counted unit has $Z = 2$, $E = 0$, $S = \ln 2$ at the Hawking temperature, the values of one zero-weight mode;
\item[(iii)] (against the hole) per counted unit $\beta E = \pi/6$ and $E/(TS) = \pi/(6\ln 2) \in (0.75539, 0.75540)$, below one as clause (v) of Theorem \claimref{synthesis.residue-from-mode-entropy} requires. Under the area-law hypothesis ($N = A/(4\ln 2)$ counted units, $T = 1/8\pi M$, Theorem \claimref{synthesis.quarter-from-temperature}) the counted units carry $E_{\mathrm{c}} = \pi M/(12\ln 2) \in (0.37769, 0.37770)\,M$, below $TS = M/2$; if the hole is the Gibbs state of hypothesis (H) of Theorem \claimref{synthesis.hole-mass-is-ground-energy-label} with the counted units as its excitations, its ground energy is $E_{0}(M) = M(1 - \pi/(12\ln 2)) \in (0.62230, 0.62231)\,M$, inside the interval $(M/2, M]$ of that theorem's (ii) and strictly below the zero-weight value $M$ of its (iii). This value is independent of the ordering, since $E_{0}$ is the bottom of the spectrum; in the symmetric ordering of (ii) the same Hamiltonian is a c-number of exactly $M$ plus counted units of mean energy zero, and the Smarr relation reads $\beta M = 2N\ln 2$ as in the zero-weight case;
\item[(iv)] (what distinguishes the two realisations of $\ln 2$) the energy fluctuation of one counted unit is $\beta^{2}\operatorname{Var}(E) \in (1.5964, 1.5965)$, against $0$ for one zero-weight mode.
\end{enumerate}
Remark (not a further clause): the exact values in (i) and (ii) come from the self-dual point $\tau = I_4$ of the modular parameter (read, as for every modular form, in $\mathrm{span}\{1, I_4\}$), where the real-time recurrence period $2\pi/\kappa$ of the array's Killing flow equals the thermal period $\beta$; the zero offset has no comparable simplification.
So at the adopted weights the counted units carry about $0.378\,M$ of excitation energy above their sea and the label carries the rest, about $0.622\,M$. Whether the counted units carry any energy at all is a matter of how the sea's energy is ordered: in the zeta-regularised symmetric ordering they carry none and the label is all of $M$. Which ordering is physical, and what structure supplies the label, are not decided here.

synthesis.eviction-keeps-one-replica-sad.formal:
Work under the hypotheses of Theorem \claimref{singularity.eviction-window-thermal-product-radiation-entropy}, with window $W(t)$, capacity $C(t) = |W(t)|\ln 2$, member marginals $\operatorname{diag}(c_{k}^{2}, s_{k}^{2})$, and the radiation $R$ purifying the window. The modes of window and radiation are the null pairs $v_{k} = \tfrac12(e_{k} + f_{k})$, $w_{k} = \tfrac12(e_{k} - f_{k})$ of a Clifford algebra with generators $e_{k}, f_{k}$, $Q(e_{k}) = 1$, $Q(f_{k}) = -1$, with mode parity $p_{k} = 1 - 2w_{k}v_{k} = f_{k}e_{k}$; a subsystem is the sub-Clifford algebra generated by its modes, reduction to it is the projection onto its blades times the dimension of the complementary subsystem, the trace of a subsystem of $a$ modes is $2^{a}\langle\cdot\rangle_{0}$, and the states are even: they commute with the product $\Gamma$ of all $p_{k}$, that is, they are fixed by $x \mapsto \tfrac12(x + \Gamma x\Gamma)$, the total-parity form of the single-mode average of Theorem \claimref{singularity.descent-parity-average}. No tensor product is used; for even states this split gives the same reduced states as the tensor product obtained from it by the twist of Theorem \claimref{tower.commuting-copies} (shown in the proof). A member's marginal is $\rho_{k} = \tfrac12(1 + r_{k}p_{k})$, $r_{k} = c_{k}^{2} - s_{k}^{2}$, in the algebra of its mode, with trace $2\langle\cdot\rangle_{0}$. Then:
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(i)] (purity at every weight) $\operatorname{tr}\rho_{R}^{2} = \operatorname{tr}\rho_{L}^{2} = \prod_{k \in W(t)}(c_{k}^{4} + s_{k}^{4})$, with $c_{k}^{4} + s_{k}^{4} = 2\langle\rho_{k}^{2}\rangle_{0} = \tfrac12(1 + r_{k}^{2})$; one product, the purity of the current window;
\item[(ii)] (zero weight: one saddle) if every member has zero weight, after $j$ radiated quanta (radiation dimension $k = 2^{j}$) at a stage where the capacity bound on the window (the stretched-singularity capacity of the rate model) is $n_{\mathrm{cap}}\ln 2$, so that $|W(t)| = \min(j, n_{\mathrm{cap}})$ and $C(t) = \min(j, n_{\mathrm{cap}})\ln 2$, the radiation purity is exactly
\[
\operatorname{tr}\rho_{R}^{2} = \max\bigl(1/k,\ e^{-n_{\mathrm{cap}}\ln 2}\bigr) ,
\]
the larger of the two terms of the two-saddle value $P_{2} := 1/k + e^{-n_{\mathrm{cap}}\ln 2}$, and never their sum;
\item[(iii)] (the random-state average) for a pure even state of $a + b$ modes, $m = 2^{a}$, $n = 2^{b}$, $a, b \ge 1$ (numbers read in $\mathrm{span}\{1, I_4\}$, conventions, the unit principle), drawn from the unitarily invariant measure of its parity sector, the reduction to the $a$ modes has $\langle\operatorname{tr}\rho_{A}^{2}\rangle = (m + n)/(mn + 2)$, which lies strictly between the one-saddle value $\max(1/m, 1/n)$ and the two-saddle value $1/m + 1/n$, below the latter by $2(m + n)/(mn(mn + 2))$. Without the parity condition, for a pure state of an abstract tensor product of spaces of dimensions $m$ and $n$, the value is $(m + n)/(mn + 1)$, which lies between the sector value and the two-saddle value, below the latter by $(m + n)/(mn(mn + 1))$;
\item[(iv)] (the crossing) at $j = n_{\mathrm{cap}}$ ($m = n$): eviction purity $1/m$, two-saddle $2/m$, random even state $2m/(m^{2} + 2)$ (abstract tensor product: $2m/(m^{2} + 1)$). In Renyi-2 entropy $S_{2} = -\ln\operatorname{tr}\rho^{2}$, the eviction branch sits at the capacity exactly, the two-saddle value exactly $\ln 2$ below it, and the random-state value below it by $\ln 2 - \ln(1 + 2m^{-2}) \in (\ln 2 - 2m^{-2}, \ln 2)$ (abstract tensor product: $\ln 2 - \ln(1 + m^{-2}) \in (\ln 2 - m^{-2}, \ln 2)$). At distance $d = |j - n_{\mathrm{cap}}|$ from the crossing the two-saddle value lies below the eviction one by $\ln(1 + 2^{-d})$;
\item[(v)] (baseline) at every weight the falling branch $S_{R} = \sum_{k \in W(t)}h_{k}$ is the thermal entropy of the window, with no correction; the deficit of clause (iii) of Theorem \claimref{synthesis.eviction-deficit-against-page-deficit}, linear in the window when the weights are bounded away from zero (as every member's is under the weights $\kappa(k + \tfrac12)$ of (Q) version 3, $|x_{k}| \geq \pi$), is measured against the window's maximal entropy $C$, which is its thermal entropy only at zero weight.
\end{enumerate}
So at Renyi index two the dictionary is term by term: the rising branch of eviction is the disconnected saddle $1/k$, the falling branch the replica-wormhole saddle $2^{-n_{\mathrm{cap}}}$, and eviction keeps whichever dominates where the replica computation adds them. The quantity on which they differ is the subdominant saddle, $\ln(1 + 2^{-d})$ in $S_{2}$, largest ($\ln 2$) at the crossing, and the random-state branch, on a parity sector or on an abstract tensor product, agrees with the two-saddle one there to $O(m^{-2})$. That the two-saddle value is the island computation's is the literature reading of the note, not a premise of the theorem.

synthesis.half-integer-weight-array-carr.formal:
Let $\{\psi_{k}\}_{k \in \Z}$ be the fermion array of Theorem \claimref{singularity.bosonisation-bridge-s-wave-sector} with evenly spaced weights $\omega_{k} = \kappa(k + \delta)$, $\delta \in \{0, \tfrac12\}$, in the quasi-free state with the marginals of (P) and (R), so that mode $k$ has $x_{k} = 2\pi\omega_{k}/\kappa = 2\pi(k + \delta)$ and entropy $s(x_{k})$ (Theorem \claimref{synthesis.boundary-mode-thermal-entropy}); write $S_{\delta} := \sum_{k \in \Z}s(x_{k})$ for the entropy of one array. The value $\delta = 0$ is the weight convention of Premise \claimref{singularity.q-joint-quasi-freeness-s-wave}; $\delta = \tfrac12$ places the sea level midway between two sites. Then:
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(i)] $S_{1/2} = 2\sum_{k \geq 0}s((2k + 1)\pi) = \tfrac12\ln 2$ exactly, by the classical identities of the Jacobi theta functions and the Dedekind eta function (Fact \claimref{synthesis.imported-theta-function-identities}: Jacobi's quartic identity $\vartheta_{3}^{4} = \vartheta_{2}^{4} + \vartheta_{4}^{4}$, the identity $\vartheta_{2}\vartheta_{3}\vartheta_{4} = 2\eta^{3}$, and the behaviour of $\vartheta_{2}, \vartheta_{3}, \vartheta_{4}, \eta$ under $\tau \mapsto -1/\tau$); without those identities, $|S_{1/2} - \tfrac12\ln 2| < 10^{-57}$ by rigorous rational bounds;
\item[(ii)] $S_{0} = \ln 2 + 2\sum_{k \geq 1}s(2\pi k) \in (0.720396698946605, 0.720396698946606)$, the value $\ln 2 + \varepsilon$ of Theorem \claimref{synthesis.residue-from-mode-entropy}(vi), and $1/(4S_{0}) \in (0.3470310, 0.3470311)$;
\item[(iii)] under the hypothesis of Theorem \claimref{synthesis.residue-from-mode-entropy} with whole arrays as the counted units, the number of arrays per Planck area per modular period is $1/(4S_{\delta})$: for $\delta = \tfrac12$ this is $1/(2\ln 2)$ (given (i); to relative accuracy $10^{-56}$ without the identities), twice the registered density $1/(4\ln 2)$ of boundary modes, so that two arrays carry the capacity $\ln 2$ of one registered boundary mode; for $\delta = 0$ it is the value in (ii), which is not a multiple $p/q$ of $1/(4\ln 2)$ with $q \le 4$.
\end{enumerate}
For $\delta = \tfrac12$ the Killing flow at one modular period is the parity automorphism (filed alongside, synthesis.q-splits-maximum-entropy-affine-weights, clause (iv)); this is the sign that one Euclidean period carries on the odd sector, $R_{2\pi} = -1$ (Proposition \claimref{state-selection.where-fermi-dirac-comes}), whereas for $\delta = 0$ the flow at one modular period is the identity. This identifies a property of the offset left open by (Q), not a derivation of the residue: which units are counted, and which offset the boundary modes have, are not decided here.

synthesis.hole-mass-is-ground-energy-lab.formal:
Assume the hypotheses of Theorem \claimref{synthesis.quarter-from-temperature}, so that along the Schwarzschild family $T = 1/8\pi M$ and $S = A/4 = 4\pi M^{2}$, hence $TS = M/2$. Suppose further (hypothesis (H)) that at each mass $M$ the hole, counted modes together with whatever else it comprises, is in the Gibbs state $\varrho_M = e^{-\beta H_M}/Z_M$, $\beta = 8\pi M$, of a self-adjoint $H_M$ bounded below with $e^{-\beta H_M}$ of trace class, with $\tr(\varrho_M H_M) = M$ and von Neumann entropy $A/4$. Write $E_0(M) := \inf\operatorname{spec}H_M$, $H'_M := H_M - E_0(M) \geq 0$ and $Z'_M := \tr e^{-\beta H'_M}$. Then:
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(i)] the free energy is $F = M - TS = M/2$, so $\ln Z_M = -A/4$: $Z_M = e^{-A/4} < 1$, the value of the Euclidean action $I = \beta M/2 = A/4$ found by Gibbons and Hawking (1977);
\item[(ii)] $M/2 < E_0(M) \leq M$, and the excitation energy satisfies $\beta\,\tr(\varrho_M H'_M) = A/4 - \ln Z'_M < A/4$, that is $\tr(\varrho_M H'_M) < TS = M/2$. In particular no sector whose Hamiltonian has ground energy zero (the counted modes with $K_k = v_k^{*}v_k$, at any weights, or an exterior field normal-ordered to its Killing vacuum and regularised to trace class) carries energy $M$, or even $M/2$, in a Gibbs state at $\beta$ whose entropy is at most $A/4$; clause (v) of Theorem \claimref{synthesis.residue-from-mode-entropy} is the case of the counted modes. The conclusion does not depend on the ordering: with the symmetric generator $\omega_k(v_k^{*}v_k - \tfrac12)$ a counted mode carries $\beta E = x(n - \tfrac12) = -\tfrac{x}{2}\tanh\tfrac{x}{2} \leq 0 < s(x)$, and the shift of ordering only moves $E_0(M)$ by the constant $-\sum_j\omega_j/2$ (filed alongside, synthesis.central-label-carries-mass-at-zero-weight, which also gives the microcanonical model of the zero-weight case);
\item[(iii)] $E_0(M) = M$ if and only if $H_M$ is the scalar $E_0(M)$ on the support of $\varrho_M$. If the hole is the counted modes $J$ of Theorem \claimref{synthesis.residue-from-mode-entropy} with $H_M = E_0(M) + \sum_{j}\omega_j v_j^{*}v_j$, then $E_0(M) = M - E$ with $E$ the counted Killing energy, and $E_0(M) = M$ exactly in the equality case $\kappa_c = 1/(4\ln 2)$, every weight zero, where $Z'_M = 2^{|J|}$ and the Smarr relation $M = 2TS$ reads $\beta E_0 = 2|J|\ln 2$;
\item[(iv)] $E_0(M)/M \in (\tfrac12, 1]$ along the family, so the ground energy is unbounded and tends to $0$ with $M$: it is a function of the state's label $M$, not a constant offset; and $H_M$ cannot be independent of $M$ on any interval of masses, since for a fixed Hamiltonian $d\langle H\rangle_\beta/d\beta = -\operatorname{Var}_\beta(H) \leq 0$ while $dM/d\beta = 1/8\pi > 0$. This is the standard negative heat capacity of the Schwarzschild family, read as a statement about the Hamiltonian.
\end{enumerate}
So under (H) the carrier of the hole's internal energy is the ground energy $E_0(M)$ of its description, a c-number label carrying more than $M/2$ at every assignment of weights and all of $M$ at zero weight. The literal tension of clause (v) excludes the equality case only if $M$ is read as excitation energy; read under (H), the equality case is the case in which the counted modes carry all the entropy and none of the energy. Which physical structure supplies $E_0(M)$ (a horizon term of Smarr-Komar type is the natural candidate) is not decided here.

synthesis.imported-theta-function-identi.formal:
Numbers are read in $\mathrm{span}\{1, I_4\}$, the complex field of scalar complex analysis (conventions, the unit principle, U1). For $\tau$ in the upper half-plane $\{x + I_4y : y > 0\}$ put $q = e^{2\pi I_4\tau}$ and
\[
\vartheta_{3}(\tau) = \sum_{n \in \Z}q^{n^{2}/2}, \quad \vartheta_{4}(\tau) = \sum_{n \in \Z}(-1)^{n}q^{n^{2}/2}, \quad \vartheta_{2}(\tau) = \sum_{n \in \Z}q^{(n + 1/2)^{2}/2}, \quad \eta(\tau) = q^{1/24}\prod_{n \geq 1}(1 - q^{n}).
\]
Then:
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] (products) $\vartheta_{3} = \prod_{n \geq 1}(1 - q^{n})(1 + q^{n - 1/2})^{2}$, $\vartheta_{4} = \prod_{n \geq 1}(1 - q^{n})(1 - q^{n - 1/2})^{2}$, $\vartheta_{2} = 2q^{1/8}\prod_{n \geq 1}(1 - q^{n})(1 + q^{n})^{2}$;
\item[(b)] (the transformation $\tau \mapsto -1/\tau$) $\vartheta_{3}(-1/\tau) = \sqrt{-I_4\tau}\,\vartheta_{3}(\tau)$, $\vartheta_{2}(-1/\tau) = \sqrt{-I_4\tau}\,\vartheta_{4}(\tau)$, $\vartheta_{4}(-1/\tau) = \sqrt{-I_4\tau}\,\vartheta_{2}(\tau)$, $\eta(-1/\tau) = \sqrt{-I_4\tau}\,\eta(\tau)$, with the branch of the square root positive on the imaginary axis $\tau = I_4y$, $y > 0$;
\item[(c)] (Jacobi's quartic identity) $\vartheta_{3}^{4} = \vartheta_{2}^{4} + \vartheta_{4}^{4}$;
\item[(d)] $\vartheta_{2}\vartheta_{3}\vartheta_{4} = 2\eta^{3}$.
\end{enumerate}
On the imaginary axis all four functions are positive. \emph{Provenance:} \cite[Ch.~21]{WhittakerWatson1927}; \cite[Ch.~I]{Mumford1983}.

synthesis.injection-freshness-is-pair-pu.formal:
Consider one injection step of the rate model of Premise \claimref{singularity.rate-models-kinematic-inputs-l-u}: the window mode $W$ and emitted quantum $R$ of the $k$-th pair, and a finite system $X$ holding everything already present, in a state $\varrho$ on $\mathcal{H}_W \otimes \mathcal{H}_R \otimes \mathcal{H}_X$ with $\mathcal{H}_W = \mathcal{H}_R = \C^{2}$, abstract qubit spaces whose numbers are read in $\mathrm{span}\{1, I_4\}$ (conventions, the unit principle, U1). Let (M) say that both single-mode marginals are the thermal marginal $\operatorname{diag}(c^{2}, s^{2})$, $c, s > 0$, $c^{2} + s^{2} = 1$, that Theorem \claimref{singularity.modular-descent} and Corollary \claimref{singularity.chains-state-class-stability} supply; (Pure) that the pair state $\varrho_{WR}$ is pure; (F) freshness, $\varrho_{WRX} = \varrho_{WR} \otimes \varrho_X$; and (TF) the thermofield form $\varrho_{WR} = |\psi\rangle\langle\psi|$, $\psi = c|00\rangle + e^{I_4\phi}s|11\rangle$. Then:
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(i)] (Pure) implies (F), for every $\varrho$;
\item[(ii)] if $\varrho_{WRX}$ is pure, clause (U) restricted to this step, then (F) implies (Pure);
\item[(iii)] under (M) with $c \neq s$, (Pure) holds if and only if (TF) does; at zero weight, $c = s$, (Pure) with (M) gives a maximally entangled pair which need not be of the form (TF), for example $(|01\rangle + |10\rangle)/\sqrt2$;
\item[(iv)] (M), (U) and the evenness (P) of Premise \claimref{singularity.p-univalence} do not imply (Pure), (F) or (TF): the state $c|00\rangle_{WR}|00\rangle_X + s|11\rangle_{WR}|11\rangle_X$, with $X$ two earlier modes, is globally pure and even, has both marginals $\operatorname{diag}(c^{2}, s^{2})$ and an even, number-diagonal pair state, and its pair state is mixed, $\tr\varrho_{WR}^{2} = c^{4} + s^{4} < 1$, and correlated with $X$.
\end{enumerate}
So for $c \neq s$ clause (I) of the rate model has exactly one content beyond the derived marginals: the pair's state is pure, equivalently (under (U)) fresh, equivalently of thermofield form. The pair form is no more supplied by the cited results than freshness is; it is the same assumption. Indeed (Pure) is hypothesis (i) of Theorem \claimref{singularity.modular-descent}, so applying that theorem to the injected pair already assumes the pair pure, hence (by (i)) fresh: clause (I) states one assumption twice. At zero weight, where the eviction theorem's maximally entangled idealisation lives (Theorem \claimref{singularity.eviction-window-thermal-product-radiation-entropy}(4)), the number-basis form needs an input beyond purity, which is hypothesis (ii) of the same theorem, invariance of the pair state under the pair's boost flow.

synthesis.killing-clock-weights-binary-r.formal:
Let the counted boundary modes of a window be $n$ completed pairs $(v_{j}, v_{j}^{*})$, with $N_{j} = v_{j}^{*}v_{j}$ and boost generators $K_{j} = \sigma_{j}/2 = N_{j} - \tfrac12$ (Proposition \claimref{hawking.modular-structure-completed-pair}), and let the Killing flow on them be $\operatorname{Ad}(e^{Jt\sum_{j}\omega_{j}K_{j}})$ in Schwarzschild time, $J = e_{2}e_{3}$ the complex unit of the completed pairs, which commutes with their mode operators (\claimref{synthesis.transverse-bivector-is-the-complex-unit-of-the-radial-atom}), with the weights $\omega_{j}$ of Definition \claimref{obj.singularity.geometric-modular-condition-g}. In rapidity $\lambda = \kappa t$ (Proposition \claimref{singularity.plane-rate}) its real generator is $X = \sum_{j}(\omega_{j}/\kappa)K_{j}$, with $\operatorname{ad}_{X}v_{j} = -(\omega_{j}/\kappa)v_{j}$ and $\operatorname{ad}_{X}v_{j}^{*} = (\omega_{j}/\kappa)v_{j}^{*}$. Assume

(K) $X$ is a clock in the sense of Lemma \claimref{scrambling.clock-inventory-boundary-modes}(ii) at unit rapidity, which is two assumptions: (K1) the nonzero adjoint eigenvalues of $X$ on the mode operators have one magnitude; (K2) that magnitude is one in the Killing flow's own rapidity $\lambda = \kappa t$, the parameter that Proposition \claimref{singularity.plane-rate} converts to Schwarzschild time. The Lemma normalises a clock's parameter by convention, which rescales $X$ and does not constrain the weights of a given flow, so (K2) is a separate assumption: Proposition \claimref{singularity.plane-rate} supports it for pairs in the boost plane, and for any other counted mode it is assumed. Under (K1) alone the statements below hold with $\pm c\kappa$ in place of $\pm\kappa$ for one $c > 0$, and the moved value $s(2\pi)$ becomes $s(2\pi c)$.

Then:
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(i)] (two weights) every counted mode has $\omega_{j} \in \{-\kappa, 0, \kappa\}$, so $x_{j} = 2\pi\omega_{j}/\kappa \in \{-2\pi, 0, 2\pi\}$: a counted mode is either moved by the clock, at $|x_{j}| = 2\pi$, or fixed by it, at $x_{j} = 0$. Without (K) the weights are free, and a diagonal generator with two distinct nonzero $|\omega_{j}|$ has no common rapidity (Lemma \claimref{scrambling.clock-inventory-boundary-modes}(iii));
\item[(ii)] (the pairs of the flow's own plane are moved) a counted mode whose ladder is a completed pair in the plane of the flow's bivector, as for the Definition's flow, whose bivector at every point of $E$ is $\sigma_{r}$, and for each mode of Definition \claimref{obj.synthesis.cosmological-horizon-boundary-modes}, a copy of the Killing plane's pair, is moved: the pair diagonalises the boost with weights $-1, +1$ (Proposition \claimref{hawking.modular-structure-completed-pair}(2)), so $\omega_{j} = \pm\kappa$. Zero weight needs a counted mode on which the Killing clock acts trivially;
\item[(iii)] (one curve) under the hypotheses of Theorem \claimref{synthesis.residue-from-mode-entropy}, with $f \in [0,1]$ the fraction of counted modes the clock moves and $s$ the mode entropy of Theorem \claimref{synthesis.boundary-mode-thermal-entropy},
\[
\kappa_{c} \;=\; \frac{1}{4\bigl((1 - f)\ln 2 + f\,s(2\pi)\bigr)} ,
\]
strictly increasing in $f$: $\kappa_{c} = 1/(4\ln 2)$, the registered value, exactly when $f = 0$, and $\kappa_{c} = 1/(4s(2\pi)) \in (18.413, 18.414)$ when $f = 1$, larger than the registered value by the factor $\ln 2/s(2\pi) \in (51.05, 51.06)$;
\item[(iv)] (binary under rotation invariance) under the hypotheses of Theorem \claimref{synthesis.residue-common-weight-particle-hole}(i) the counted modes share one weight, so $f \in \{0, 1\}$ (this split is that theorem's; (K) fixes the moved value at $x = 2\pi$): the residue is $1/(4\ln 2)$ if the Killing clock fixes the counted modes and $1/(4s(2\pi))$ if it moves them, and by (ii) the second case holds when the counted modes are completed pairs of the boost planes;
\item[(v)] ((Q) is not a clock) the weights $\omega_{k} = \kappa k$ of Premise \claimref{singularity.q-joint-quasi-freeness-s-wave} on an array $k = -j, \ldots, j$ form a clock for $j = 1$ and for no $j \geq 2$: on an array with some $|k| \geq 2$ the s-wave weights and (K) are incompatible;
\item[(vi)] (de Sitter) for the boundary modes of the cosmological horizon, $\omega_{j} > 0$ by Definition \claimref{obj.synthesis.cosmological-horizon-boundary-modes}, and (K) gives $\omega_{j} = \kappa_{\Lambda}$ for every $j$; under the hypothesis of Theorem \claimref{synthesis.residue-from-mode-entropy}(iv), $|J| = \pi N^{2}/s(2\pi)$, with $|J|/(\pi N^{2}) \in (73.65, 73.66)$.
\end{enumerate}
So (K) fixes the weights, which the Definition leaves free, up to one binary choice per mode, and the question which modes are counted becomes whether the Killing clock moves them. This does not derive the residue: (K) is a hypothesis that the series does not make, (iii), (iv) and (vi) inherit the area-law hypothesis, and which modes are counted stays open.

synthesis.light-ray-good-part-of-a-spino.formal:
Work in the spacetime algebra $\Cl(1,3)$, $\gamma_{0}^{2} = 1$, $\gamma_{i}^{2} = -1$, with $I = \gamma_{0}\gamma_{1}\gamma_{2}\gamma_{3}$ and $\sigma_{3} = \gamma_{3}\gamma_{0}$, at a point of a horizon generator in a gauge with $\hb(x_{0}) = 1$, $\Omega(x_{0}) = 0$ there. Let the generators of the null plane $N_{k} = \{x : \ip{x}{k} = 0\}$ run along $k = \gamma_{0} + \gamma_{3}$ (any future null direction is carried to this one by a rotor), let $m = \gamma_{0} - \gamma_{3}$ be the second null direction of the plane of $k$ and $\gamma_{0}$, and let $\Lambda(a) = a\gamma_{0}/(2a_{0})$ (Theorem \claimref{field-theory.second-quantisation}(1)), so $\Lambda(k) = (1 + B)/2$ and $\Lambda(m) = (1 - B)/2$ with $B = \gamma_{3}\gamma_{0}$ the boost bivector of the generator ($B^{2} = 1$, $e^{B\lambda/2}k\,e^{-B\lambda/2} = e^{\lambda}k$). Spinors are even multivectors $\psi \in \Cl^{+}(1,3)$ (real dimension $8$), on which a rotor $R$ acts by $\psi \mapsto R\psi$. Let $J = \gamma_{1}\gamma_{2} = -IB$ be the bivector of the transverse plane, $L_{X}\psi = X\psi$, $i_{H} = R_{I\sigma_{3}}$ Hestenes' unit, and $\Gamma\psi = I\psi I\sigma_{3}$ the chirality. Then:
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(i)] (the canonical part on $N_{k}$) the flux of the Dirac current $J_{\psi} = \psi\gamma_{0}\tilde{\psi}$ through $N_{k}$ is $J_{\psi}\cdot k = 2\,|\Lambda(m)\psi|^{2}$, the squared Euclidean norm in the blade basis: it vanishes identically on the image of $\Lambda(k)$ and is positive definite on the image of $\Lambda(m)$, which is $\ker L_{m}$, the $(-1)$-eigenspace of $L_{B}$, of real dimension $4$. Symmetrically the flux through $N_{m}$ is $2|\Lambda(k)\psi|^{2}$ (Theorem \claimref{synthesis.canonical-part-of-a-spinor-on-a-null-plane}(i), (ii)). So the canonical part of a spinor field on $N_{k}$ is $\Lambda(m)\psi$; $\Lambda(k)$ is the spinor of plane waves of momentum $k$ (Theorem \claimref{field-theory.second-quantisation}(1), (2)), which are constant along the generators;
\item[(ii)] (its boost weight) the covariant boost $B_{\lambda} = \cosh\tfrac{\lambda}{2} + B\sinh\tfrac{\lambda}{2}$ acts on the values of $\Lambda(m)\psi$ by $e^{-\lambda/2}$ and on those of $\Lambda(k)\psi$ by $e^{+\lambda/2}$, while the transport $u \mapsto e^{-\lambda}u$ of the generators' affine parameter multiplies the delta kernel by $e^{\lambda}$. The anticommutator is therefore preserved on the canonical part, the unitary dilation $\psi_{m}(u) \mapsto e^{-\lambda/2}\psi_{m}(e^{-\lambda}u)$, which at $\lambda = -\kappa t$ is the boost $\beta_{t}(\psi(u)) = e^{\kappa t/2}\psi(e^{\kappa t}u)$ of the continuum carrier (Theorem \claimref{synthesis.null-vacuum-on-a-horizon-generator}(i)), of weight $\tfrac12$; on $\Lambda(k)\psi$ it is scaled by $e^{2\lambda}$;
\item[(iii)] (two chirality channels) $\Gamma^{2} = 1$, $\Gamma$ commutes with every $L_{X}$, $X \in \Cl^{+}(1,3)$ (so it is Lorentz invariant), with $i_{H}$ and with the central unit $I$ of Theorem \claimref{field-theory.where-each-lives}; its eigenspaces $W_{\pm}$ (the Weyl spinors) have real dimension $4$ and meet the canonical part in real dimension $2$ each: the canonical part is one complex channel of each chirality, under either unit;
\item[(iv)] (helicity) on the canonical part $L_{J} = -\Gamma\,i_{H}$ and $L_{J} = L_{I}$; the transverse rotation $e^{-J\phi/2}$ acts on the channel of chirality $\epsilon$ as $e^{+\epsilon i_{H}\phi/2}$: helicity about the generator direction $k$ is minus the chirality, equivalently the chirality about $m$. On the image of $\Lambda(k)$ the opposite signs hold, and neither identity holds on all of $\Cl^{+}(1,3)$. With the central unit both canonical channels are complex-linear for $L_{J} = L_{I}$, the other chirality's particle content entering conjugated, the split of Theorem \claimref{field-theory.second-quantisation}(4);
\item[(v)] (Weyl against Dirac) a Weyl spinor field has one complex canonical channel on each light ray of $N_{k}$, a Hestenes (Dirac) spinor field two, one of each chirality and so of opposite helicities;
\item[(vi)] (contrast) vector and bivector fields carry the boost by conjugation $X \mapsto RX\tilde{R}$ with integer weights ($\gamma_{1}, \gamma_{2}$ weight $0$; $k$, $k\wedge\gamma_{1}$, $k\wedge\gamma_{2}$ weight $1$; $m$ weight $-1$); only the one-sided spinor action gives the square root, and on spinors a full turn in the transverse plane is the rotor $J^{2} = -1$;
\item[(vii)] (the count) read with one array of version 3 of Premise (Q), weights $\kappa(k + \tfrac12)$, per complex canonical channel on a light ray, a massless Dirac species carries two arrays and a Weyl field one, each $\tfrac12\ln 2$ at the Hawking temperature (Theorem \claimref{synthesis.half-integer-weight-array-carries-half-ln2}(i)). With the count per light ray and the counted unit a bookkeeping total over all horizon fields, a light ray carrying $n_{D}$ Dirac species and $n_{W}$ further Weyl fields carries $2n_{D} + n_{W}$ arrays, $(n_{D} + \tfrac12 n_{W})\ln 2$, and a registered unit of $\ln 2$ is two arrays of this total; one Dirac species is exactly one such unit. Under the hypothesis of Theorem \claimref{synthesis.residue-from-mode-entropy} the residue per unit of two arrays is the registered $1/(4\ln 2)$. \emph{Note.} For the series' matter the slot module excludes a fifteen-component generation: its light-ray arrays are its Weyl components, sixteen per generation for every CPT halving and every mass term, eight registered units (Theorem \claimref{synthesis.slot-module-light-ray-arrays-are-weyl-components}), so the half unit of a Weyl-only neutrino, a species with no right-handed partner, does not occur there.
\end{enumerate}
So the spinor reading that fixes the offset of (Q) version 3 also fixes the channel count: each complex canonical channel on a light ray is one chirality of one spinor field, and two arrays, the registered unit, are the light-ray content of one massless Dirac species, with helicity about the generators equal to minus the chirality. (i) to (vi) are identities in $\Cl(1,3)$ and use neither the area law nor any thermal premise; the area law enters (vii) only to state the residue.

synthesis.number-diagonal-dynamics-sprea.formal:
Let $v_{1}, \dots, v_{n}$ satisfy the CAR, let $\sigma_{k} = v_{k}^{*}v_{k} - v_{k}v_{k}^{*}$ (Theorem \claimref{singularity.modular-structure-completed-pair}), and let $\mathcal{D}$ be the abelian algebra the $\sigma_{k}$ generate, the joint-number-diagonal algebra. Numbers are read in $\mathrm{span}\{1, J\}$, $J = e_{2}e_{3}$ the complex unit of the completed pairs, which commutes with their mode operators (\claimref{synthesis.transverse-bivector-is-the-complex-unit-of-the-radial-atom}); the coupling constants are written $g_{jk}$. Let $H = H^{*} \in \mathcal{D}$ and, for a mode $j$, write uniquely $H = H_{0}^{(j)} + \sigma_{j}H_{1}^{(j)}$ with $H_{0}^{(j)}, H_{1}^{(j)}$ in the algebra of $\{\sigma_{k} : k \neq j\}$; let $N(j)$, the coupling neighbourhood of $j$, be the set of modes on which $H_{1}^{(j)}$ depends. Then:
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(i)] $\operatorname{ad}_{H}^{m}(v_{j}) = v_{j}(-2H_{1}^{(j)})^{m}$ for every $m \ge 0$, and the phase flow gives $e^{JtH}v_{j}e^{-JtH} = v_{j}\,e^{-2JtH_{1}^{(j)}}$ for every real $t$: the evolved mode operator is $v_{j}$ times a diagonal unitary of the modes of $N(j)$;
\item[(ii)] its support never leaves $\{j\}\cup N(j)$: for every mode $l \notin \{j\}\cup N(j)$ and every $t$, $e^{JtH}v_{j}e^{-JtH}$ anticommutes with $v_{l}$ and $v_{l}^{*}$, so every out-of-time-order correlator of $v_{j}$ with a mode outside its coupling neighbourhood is independent of $t$;
\item[(iii)] if $H_{1}^{(j)} = h_{j} + \sum_{k \in N(j)}g_{jk}\sigma_{k}$, then $e^{JtH}v_{j}e^{-JtH} = e^{-2Jh_{j}t}\,v_{j}\prod_{k \in N(j)}(\cos 2g_{jk}t - J\sin 2g_{jk}t\,\sigma_{k})$; in the trace inner product the operators $v_{j}\sigma_{S}$, $S \subseteq N(j)$, are orthogonal of equal norm, the weight on size $|S|$ is $\prod_{k \notin S}\cos^{2}2g_{jk}t\prod_{k \in S}\sin^{2}2g_{jk}t$, and the mean size $\sum_{k \in N(j)}\sin^{2}2g_{jk}t$ is an almost periodic function, at most $|N(j)|$, vanishing at $t = 0$; with $n - 1$ uniform couplings $g$ it is $(n-1)\sin^{2}2gt$, which first reaches half the window at $t = \pi/(8g)$ for every $n$;
\item[(iv)] by contrast a generator outside $\mathcal{D}$ does propagate: with a hop on the chain $1$-$2$-$3$ added to $\sigma_{0}\sigma_{1}$, $\operatorname{ad}^{m}(v_{0})$ first involves mode $m$ at order $m$.
\end{enumerate}
Since every generator (L$_{E}$) admits lies in $\mathcal{D}$ (Proposition \claimref{singularity.number-diagonal-dynamics-models-scrambling-derived}), no dynamics the locality premise admits, quadratic or not, carries operator support beyond direct couplings in real time, and none produces exponential growth: the mean size, the matrix elements of every local commutator and every out-of-time-order correlator of $v_{j}$ are finite sums of terms $e^{J\omega t}$, almost periodic and bounded. With all-to-all couplings the support does grow to the whole window, the mean size reaching half the window in time $\pi/(8g)$, independent of $n$. The exponential growth step of Proposition \claimref{scrambling.spreading-law-chaos-bound-saturated}, and with it the law $t_{*} = \kappa^{-1}\ln N$, therefore has no real-time reading inside (L$_{E}$); it needs a generator that (L$_{E}$) excludes.

synthesis.residue-common-weight-particle.formal:
Let the counted boundary modes be $n$ completed pairs $\psi_{1}, \ldots, \psi_{n}$ whose one-particle space $\C^{n}$ carries the principal $\mathfrak{su}(2)$ of Theorem \claimref{scrambling.principality-indifference-sphere} (its generators written $L_{a}$ here, so as not to clash with the unit $J$; numbers on the modes and on $\C^{n}$ are read in $\mathrm{span}\{1, J\}$, $J = e_{2}e_{3}$ the complex unit of the completed pairs, which commutes with their mode operators, \claimref{synthesis.transverse-bivector-is-the-complex-unit-of-the-radial-atom}), with $E_{ij} = \psi_{i}^{\dagger}\psi_{j}$ (Proposition \claimref{scrambling.boundary-mode-space-matrix}), $N_{\mathrm{tot}} = \sum_{i}E_{ii}$, $\hat{X} := \sum_{ij}X_{ij}E_{ij}$ for $X \in \Mat_{n}(\C)$, and $\mathcal{A}_{0}$ the algebra generated by the $E_{ij}$. For $0 < q < 1$ let $\rho_{q}$ be the gauge-invariant quasi-free state with symbol $q\,\id$ (Fact \claimref{hawking.imported-quasi-free-states-car-algebra}). Then:
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(i)] (one weight, one occupation) a number-preserving quasi-free generator $\hat{h}$ commutes with every $\hat{L}_{a}$ if and only if $h = \omega\,\id$, so $\hat{h} = \omega N_{\mathrm{tot}}$; and a gauge-invariant quasi-free state invariant under the rotations has symbol $q\,\id$, so it is $\rho_{q}$. With rotation invariance imposed, every counted mode has the same Killing weight $\omega$ and the same occupation, and under the hypotheses of Theorem \claimref{synthesis.residue-from-mode-entropy} the residue is $\kappa_{c} = 1/(4s(x))$ for the single value $x = 2\pi\omega/\kappa$ (Theorem \claimref{synthesis.boundary-mode-thermal-entropy}). Without quasi-freeness this fails: the commutant of the rotations on the Fock space is larger than $\operatorname{span}\{\id, N_{\mathrm{tot}}\}$;
\item[(ii)] (the weight is invisible on the gauge-invariant algebra) $N_{\mathrm{tot}}$ is central in $\mathcal{A}_{0}$, so the flow of $\omega N_{\mathrm{tot}}$ restricts to the identity on $\mathcal{A}_{0}$ for every $\omega$, and for every $q$ the restriction of $\rho_{q}$ to $\mathcal{A}_{0}$ is tracial, hence KMS at every $\beta$ for the restricted flow, while $\rho_{q}(N_{\mathrm{tot}}) = nq$ separates them. Neither the boost's action on the gauge-invariant bilinears nor any KMS condition posed on $\mathcal{A}_{0}$ determines $q$; in particular the inference from the trivial restriction to zero weight, and so to $\ln 2$ per mode, is invalid;
\item[(iii)] (particle-hole) let $\varepsilon$ be the $\mathfrak{su}(2)$-invariant bilinear form of the principal representation, symmetric for integer $j$ and symplectic for half-integer $j$, with matrix $\varepsilon_{m,m'} = (-1)^{j - m}\delta_{m,-m'}$ in the standard basis of spin $j = (n-1)/2$, a signed permutation and hence orthogonal. The Bogoliubov automorphism $C(\psi_{i}) = \sum_{k}\varepsilon_{ik}\psi_{k}^{\dagger}$ fixes every $\hat{L}_{a}$, maps $N_{\mathrm{tot}}$ to $n - N_{\mathrm{tot}}$, and satisfies $\rho_{q}\circ C = \rho_{1-q}$; $C^{2}(\psi_{i}) = \sum_{l}(\varepsilon^{2})_{il}\psi_{l}$, so $C$ is an involution for integer $j$ and squares to the parity automorphism $\psi \mapsto -\psi$ for half-integer $j$. So $\rho_{q}$ is $C$-invariant exactly when $q = \tfrac12$, that is exactly at zero common weight; under the hypotheses of Theorem \claimref{synthesis.residue-from-mode-entropy} and (i), the registered residue $1/(4\ln 2)$ holds if and only if the counted state is invariant under $C$. The direction from $C$-invariance to the registered value needs no quasi-freeness: a rotation-invariant gauge-invariant state has two-point matrix $q\,\id$, and $C$-invariance then forces $q = 1 - q = \tfrac12$, maximally mixed marginals;
\item[(iv)] ((Q) on a principal window) on $n = 2j + 1$ modes the weights $\omega_{k} = \kappa k$, $k = -j, \ldots, j$, of Premise \claimref{singularity.q-joint-quasi-freeness-s-wave} are $n$ distinct values, excluded by (i); they are the spectrum of $\kappa h/2$ for the Cartan element $h$ of the principal triple, a rotation generator, which does not commute with the rotations.
\end{enumerate}
If, further, the counted modes obey Definition \claimref{obj.hawking.boundary-mode-algebra-windows-boost-dynamics}, whose frequencies are strictly positive, (i) and Theorem \claimref{synthesis.residue-from-mode-entropy}(ii) give $\kappa_{c} > 1/(4\ln 2)$ strictly at the hole, as clause (iv) of that theorem gives at the cosmological horizon. The hypotheses here (the counted modes are the principal mode space; the counted state and the Killing generator restricted to it are rotation invariant and quasi-free) are stated, not derived; that the counted modes are the fuzzy-sphere modes is the provenance question.

synthesis.residue-from-mode-entropy.formal:
Assume (P) (Premise \claimref{singularity.p-univalence}) and (R) (Premise \claimref{singularity.r-regularity-fixed-locus}). Let $J$ be the boundary modes of a Schwarzschild hole registered in one modular period, of Killing weights $\omega_{j}$, and suppose that they carry the hole's thermodynamic entropy: with $s$ the thermal entropy of one mode (Theorem \claimref{synthesis.boundary-mode-thermal-entropy}) and $S = A/4$ the entropy that the series' temperature fixes (Theorem \claimref{synthesis.quarter-from-temperature}),
\[
\sum_{j \in J} s(x_{j}) \;=\; \frac{A}{4}, \qquad x_{j} = \frac{2\pi\omega_{j}}{\kappa} .
\]
Write $\kappa_{c} := |J|/A$ for the residue, the counted modes per Planck area of horizon per modular period, and $\langle s\rangle := |J|^{-1}\sum_{j} s(x_{j})$. Then:
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(i)] $\kappa_{c} = 1/(4\langle s\rangle)$: the residue is the reciprocal of four times the counted modes' mean thermal entropy;
\item[(ii)] $\kappa_{c} \ge 1/(4\ln 2)$, with equality if and only if every counted mode has zero Killing weight. The capacity-convention residue of T08, and with it the registered value $\nu = 1/(16\pi\ln 2)$ of the boundary-mode prediction, is therefore, under this hypothesis, equivalent to the counted modes being at zero weight, maximally mixed; a count that includes modes of nonzero weight needs more of them;
\item[(iii)] $\ln 2$ enters the residue as the entropy of a maximally mixed fermion, as T08's own remark on $\nu$ records, not through any count, which is consistent with T08's proof that no rational recount of the exceptional set reaches $1/(4\ln 2)$;
\item[(iv)] the same holds at the cosmological horizon under (P) and (R$_{\Lambda}$) (Premise \claimref{synthesis.r-lambda-regularity-cosmological-horizon}), with $S_{\Lambda} = \pi N^{2}$ (Theorem \claimref{synthesis.de-sitter-entropy-pi-n-squared}) and the temperature $\kappa_{\Lambda}/2\pi$ (Theorem \claimref{synthesis.de-sitter-temperature-frozen-point-norm}) in place of the hole's: if the cosmological horizon's counted modes carry its entropy, $|J| > \pi N^{2}/\ln 2$, strictly, since the Definition \claimref{obj.synthesis.cosmological-horizon-boundary-modes} requires $\omega_{j} > 0$; under the hypothesis the capacity value of Theorem \claimref{synthesis.de-sitter-mode-count} is then excluded;
\item[(v)] (the energy tension) for $x > 0$, $\beta\omega n < s(x)$, so the counted modes' Killing excitation energy $E$ satisfies $\beta E < \sum_{j} s(x_{j}) = A/4$, that is $E < TS = M/2$, and $E = 0$ in the equality case of (ii): the internal energy $M$ of Theorem \claimref{synthesis.quarter-from-temperature} is not the counted modes' energy, and read literally the two hypotheses together exclude the equality case of (ii);
\item[(vi)] (Premise (Q) taken literally) with the weights $\omega_{k} = \kappa k$ of (Q), one zero-weight mode per array and the rest at $x = 2\pi|k|$, an array carries $\ln 2 + \varepsilon$ nats, $\varepsilon = 2\sum_{k \ge 1} s(2\pi k) \in (0.02724, 0.02726)$, so $\ln 2 + \varepsilon \in (0.7203, 0.7205)$. Counting every mode of an array drives $\langle s\rangle$ to $0$ and $\kappa_{c}$ upwards; counting arrays gives $1/(4(\ln 2 + \varepsilon)) \in (0.3469, 0.3471)$ arrays per Planck area per period, against the registered $1/(4\ln 2) = 0.3607$; counting only the zero-weight modes leaves $\varepsilon$ nats per array outside $A/4$. So no reading of (Q) reproduces the registered value.
\end{enumerate}
Under this hypothesis the residue is equivalent to the Killing weights of the counted modes, with the registered value as its zero-weight extreme. The hypothesis $\sum_{j} s(x_{j}) = A/4$ is the area-law condition that T08's circularity fence holds under test, so this is a conditional reformulation, not an admissible derivation of the residue from the structure of the boundary modes, and it neither meets nor fails the kill condition of the boundary-mode prediction. By (vi), were Premise (Q) promoted to a derivation, it would return a residue other than the registered one, and the kill condition would then refute (IC). The weights, and which modes are counted, remain open.

synthesis.sds-clausius-fixes-area-consta.formal:
Let $\Lambda = 3/\ell^{2} > 0$, in units $\hbar = c = G = 1$, and for $0 < M < M_{N} := \ell/(3\sqrt{3})$ consider the Schwarzschild-de Sitter gravitational field on flat spacetime (Definition \claimref{obj.synthesis.gtg-flat-spacetime-gauge-fields}) in the Painlev\'e-Gullstrand gauge $\hb = 1 - \beta P$, $\beta^{2} = 2M/r + r^{2}/\ell^{2}$, whose static Killing field $K = \hb^{-1}(e_t)$ has $Q(K) = f(r) = 1 - 2M/r - r^{2}/\ell^{2}$. The positive zeros of $f$ are the black-hole horizon locus $r_{b}$ and the cosmological horizon locus $r_{c}$, with $0 < r_{b} < \ell/\sqrt{3} < r_{c} < \ell$ (the third zero is $-(r_{b} + r_{c})$), whose cross-sections in the chart have areas $A_{h} = 4\pi r_{h}^{2}$. Suppose
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(T)] each horizon is at the temperature $T_{h} = |f'(r_{h})|/4\pi$ of the Killing flow $\partial_{t}$ (the flow of $K$), the value that (R) gives at $\Lambda = 0$ (Theorem \claimref{synthesis.quarter-from-temperature}) and (R$_{\Lambda}$) gives at $M = 0$ (Theorem \claimref{synthesis.de-sitter-temperature-frozen-point-norm}), here assumed on both horizons of the whole family;
\item[(Cl)] each horizon obeys the Clausius relation along the family at fixed $\Lambda$, $dM = T_{b}\,dS_{b}$ and $dM = -T_{c}\,dS_{c}$ (the cosmological horizon shrinks as $M$ grows), as in Hawking's argument.
\end{enumerate}
Then:
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(i)] $S_{b} = A_{b}/4 + c_{b}$ and $S_{c} = A_{c}/4 + c_{c}$ with $c_{b}$, $c_{c}$ independent of $M$ (they may depend on $\Lambda$), and every pair of such constants is compatible with (T) and (Cl): the temperatures fix the coefficient $\tfrac14$ on both horizons and nothing else.
\item[(ii)] (Z) $S_{b} \to 0$ as $M \to 0$, the normalisation of Theorem \claimref{synthesis.quarter-from-temperature}, holds if and only if $c_{b} = 0$.
\item[(iii)] (N) Continuity at the Nariai merger, $\lim S_{b} = \lim S_{c}$ as $M \to M_{N}$, where both radii tend to $\ell/\sqrt{3}$, holds if and only if $c_{c} = c_{b}$.
\item[(iv)] Under (Z) and (N), as $M \to 0$ the cosmological horizon's entropy tends to $\pi\ell^{2} = \pi N^{2}$, with $N^{2} = M_{P}^{2}/Q(I_{\Lambda})$ (Fact \claimref{standard-model.imported-frozen-point-dictionary}): the value of Theorem \claimref{synthesis.de-sitter-entropy-pi-n-squared} is reached from the series' temperatures and the black hole's normalisation, without the imported area law.
\item[(v)] An entropy that adds one constant $c \neq 0$ to every spherical horizon of the family satisfies (T), (Cl) and (N) and violates (Z). Since $S_{b} \to c$ as $M \to 0$, nonnegativity of the black hole's entropy alone excludes every $c < 0$; an entropy that is the von Neumann entropy of the horizon's boundary modes is nonnegative (each mode carries $s \ge 0$, Theorem \claimref{synthesis.boundary-mode-thermal-entropy}). (Z) fixes $c = 0$ exactly. The Gauss-Bonnet term of the MacDowell-Mansouri action as written (Theorem \claimref{dark-energy.frozen-point-makes-stelle-west-vector}) contributes a negative constant of this kind under the hypothesis that horizon entropy is the Wald entropy, whose Jacobson-Myers form is Fact \claimref{synthesis.imported-wald-noether-charge-entropy}; its value and the resulting bound on Schwarzschild-de Sitter black holes are computed in the result filed alongside, synthesis.euler-term-horizon-entropy-shift. Under (T), (Cl) and (Z), or under nonnegativity, that contribution must be removed from horizon entropy.
\end{enumerate}
